\section{\code{BankMarket}}{

\code{BankMarket} is the bank-family analogue of \code{LogitDebt}: it holds the
bank-level Nash-in-rates pricing state (ownership, rates, marginal-cost
bookkeeping -- all inherited from \code{Antitrust}) PLUS the row-level
bank-market panel and liquidity-friction primitives needed to reconstruct
conditional shares and price the liquidity shadow cost \code{Lambda}
(\code{BankRisk.R}'s \code{calc_portfolio_liquidity()}).

Ownership (\code{ownerPre}/\code{ownerPost}/\code{labels}, inherited from \code{Antitrust}) is
at the BANK level: length \code{J} (the number of active banks), one label per
bank. \code{pricePre}/\code{pricePost}/\code{mcPre}/\code{mcPost} (also inherited, \code{numeric})
are length \verb{2J}: the first \code{J} elements are loan rates \code{r_L}, the second
\code{J} are deposit rates \code{r_D}, per bank, in the same bank order as \code{labels}.
\code{Antitrust}'s own validity method only constrains \code{labels}/\code{ownerPre}/
\code{ownerPost} to a common length (verified experimentally against the
antitrust@refactor library, \verb{0.99.38}: \code{pricePre}/\code{mcPre} length is NOT
separately validated), so this \verb{2J}-length convention is compatible with
\code{contains = "Antitrust"} without friction -- see the migration report for
the verification.

The row-level bank-market panel (\code{marketID}, \code{bankRow}, \code{marketSize_L}/
\verb{_D}, \code{sCondBase_L}/\verb{_D}, \code{rBase_L}/\verb{_D}, \code{alpha_L}/\verb{_D}, \code{outsideMu_L}/
\verb{_D}, \code{phiOut_L}/\verb{_D}, \code{costRaw_L}/\verb{_D}, \code{riskFreeRate}) is length \code{N}
(bank-market rows), analogous to \code{stability}'s \code{df_year} panel in
\code{simulate_merger_exposure.R}, but WITHOUT that script's Stan-panel-specific
\code{ref_bank_id}/\code{ref_row_id}/\code{out_id} bookkeeping (institution-bound
plumbing tied to the original G-group Stan panel -- out of migration
scope per \code{design/ARCHITECTURE.md}'s "No DB/FRED/QCEW ingestion"). Instead,
each market's conditional-share utility is normalized against that
market's FIRST row (the same \code{normIndex}-relative meanval-inversion
convention \code{LogitDebt}/\code{logit.debt()} already uses -- see
\code{DebtEquilibrium.R}'s \code{calcSlopes}), rather than a caller-supplied
reference row. This is a representational simplification for a
fresh/portable caller (see the migration report's "Z_psi-without-
original-panel" discussion); the underlying conditional-logit-with-
liquidity-shifted-marginal-cost economics are unchanged.
